1. Redis Caching
Today: server/src/core/cache/ uses an in-memory implementation behind a cache interface.
Future: add a Redis implementation of the same interface (drop-in, no changes to modules).
Candidate uses: permission lookups, settings, announcements, rate limiting, session/refresh-token blacklist, Socket.IO adapter for multiple server instances.
Trigger: more than one server instance, or measurable DB load.
2. Docker, Kubernetes, Containers
Basic Dockerfiles (docker/) exist for local/dev use.
Future: production-grade images (multi-stage, small, non-root), container registry, health probes using /health and readiness endpoints.
Kubernetes manifests/Helm charts in infra/k8s/: deployments, services, ingress, config maps, secrets, autoscaling (HPA).
3. Microservices Extraction
Modules are written so they can be extracted: each has its own routes, service, repository, validation, events.
Extract a module only when it needs independent scaling (e.g. notifications, search, files).
Steps per module: move to its own service → replace internal event bus with a message broker → replace direct service calls with API/queue calls → give it its own DB schema if needed.
Candidates: notifications/realtime, search, files, audit-logs.
4. Message Queue / Background Jobs
Move scheduled and heavy jobs (email, WhatsApp, announcement expiry, cleanup) to a queue (e.g. BullMQ with Redis, or SQS on AWS).
4b. Circuit Breakers
What: a protection pattern. When a dependency (external API or a microservice) keeps failing or timing out, the circuit "opens" and calls fail fast (or use a fallback) instead of waiting and piling up. After a cool-down it lets a few test calls through ("half-open") and closes again if they succeed.
Why: stops one slow/failing service from freezing the whole app, which matters with up to 1000 concurrent users.
Where to apply:
External integrations: WhatsApp (wa.officall.ceoitbox.com), Google/Outlook/SMTP email, Google Maps, Cloudinary.
Calls between services after microservices extraction (see section 3).
Behavior to define when building: failure threshold, timeout, cool-down time, fallback response (e.g. queue the message and retry later, show a friendly error), and log/alert when a circuit opens.
How: wrap provider calls in server/src/integrations/* with a circuit breaker (e.g. the opossum library), with settings in config/ (no hardcoding).
Trigger: before going live with heavy external integration use, or at the time of microservices extraction.
5. AWS Migration (after the project runs successfully)
Compute: ECS/EKS or EC2 for server; S3 + CloudFront for client.
Database: RDS MySQL.
Secrets: AWS Secrets Manager / SSM Parameter Store.
Cache: ElastiCache (Redis).
Logs/metrics: CloudWatch.
Optional IaC: Terraform in infra/aws/.
Deployment guide in docs/deployment/.
5b. Combined Deployment (optional)
Client and server are deployed separately at first. Serving the client from the server (or one container) can be decided later if needed.
6. Observability Upgrades
Centralized logs, tracing (OpenTelemetry), dashboards and alert rules.
7. Scaling & Performance
Read replicas, DB indexing review, CDN for static assets, load testing for the 1000 concurrent user target.
8. Security Hardening
Penetration test, dependency scanning, WAF, secret rotation, audit review.

